 "notes": "Tolerance: the P1 seed spread is 0 by coincidence (each of the three 50-pair draws agreed on 41 of 50); the binomial standard error at n = 50 is 5.4 points, so the 2x spread rule is vacuous for this lab. P2 (change.agreement_pct minus P1.agreement_pct, -4 points) is reported as within noise: 3 pairs right to wrong, 1 wrong to right, two-sided exact sign test p = 0.625. P3's effect is the flip rate itself, against the 0 flips of an order-blind judge (mkanswers takes two names, so this is checked by hand): 7 of 50 pairs flipped, 6 toward the answer shown first and 1 toward the second (from picks_a 54 to 44); 4 flips turned a wrong verdict right and 3 a right verdict wrong (from agreement 82 to 84); counting a win only when it holds in both orders gives 38 of 50 (76%). The judge is deterministic here: the 25 pairs that two baseline draws share (6 + 10 + 9), judged in separate runs in the same order, got identical verdicts, so every flip comes from the order. Offline, from the dataset and the saved seed-0 verdicts: all eligible pairs together have 89.34% human-human agreement over 694 vote pairs; seed 0 by model pair: strong vs 13B 28 of 32 right, strong vs strong 3 of 6, 13B vs 13B 10 of 12 (strong = gpt-4, claude-v1, gpt-3.5-turbo). The spec's whole-file human-human figures reproduce exactly from the parquet. Seconds per item depend on load: baseline seed 0 ran beside other jobs (4.07712 s per item) while break seed 0 sent the same prompt lengths at 2.06231 s."
